### What is shallow copy vs deep copy?

The difference lies in how mutable nested objects are handled.

### What does a shallow copy look like?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Account {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private Address address; // mutable nested object

    public Account shallowCopy() {
        Account copy = new Account();
        copy.accountId = this.accountId;   // String is immutable: safe
        copy.balance = this.balance;        // BigDecimal is immutable: safe
        copy.address = this.address;        // Reference copied: SAME object
        return copy;
    }
}

Account original = new Account("SAV2001", balance, new Address("42 MG Road", "Mumbai"));
Account shallow = original.shallowCopy();

shallow.getAddress().setCity("Delhi");
System.out.println(original.getAddress().getCity()); // "Delhi": original changed!
// Both point to the SAME Address object.
```

### What does a deep copy look like?

```java
public class Account {
    public Account deepCopy() {
        Account copy = new Account();
        copy.accountId = this.accountId;   // immutable: safe
        copy.balance = this.balance;        // immutable: safe
        copy.address = new Address(         // NEW Address: independent
            this.address.getStreet(),
            this.address.getCity()
        );
        return copy;
    }
}

Account deep = original.deepCopy();
deep.getAddress().setCity("Delhi");
System.out.println(original.getAddress().getCity()); // "Mumbai": original unchanged!
```

### What are the three ways to deep copy?

```java
// Way 1: Copy constructor (recommended)
public class Account {
    public Account(Account source) {
        this.accountId = source.accountId;
        this.balance = source.balance;
        this.address = new Address(source.address); // Address also has copy constructor
    }
}

// Way 2: Cloneable + clone() override (discouraged)
public class Account implements Cloneable {
    @Override
    public Account clone() {
        try {
            Account copy = (Account) super.clone(); // shallow copy
            copy.address = new Address(this.address); // manually deep copy nested
            return copy;
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    }
}
// Problems: Cloneable is a marker (no method), super.clone() is shallow, checked exception.

// Way 3: Serialization (automatic but slow)
public static <T extends java.io.Serializable> T deepCopy(T object) {
    try {
        var bos = new java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream();
        new java.io.ObjectOutputStream(bos).writeObject(object);
        var bis = new java.io.ByteArrayInputStream(bos.toByteArray());
        return (T) new java.io.ObjectInputStream(bis).readObject();
    } catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
}
// Handles any depth automatically but slow and requires Serializable.
```

### Why are immutable objects safe in shallow copies?

```java
// String, BigDecimal, LocalDate are immutable.
// Shallow copy shares the reference but the object cannot be modified.
Account shallow = original.shallowCopy();
// shallow.accountId and original.accountId point to same String "SAV2001"
// But String is immutable: nobody can change it. Sharing is safe.
// Only MUTABLE nested objects (Address, List<Transaction>) need deep copying.
```

### How do shallow and deep copy compare?

| Aspect | Shallow Copy | Deep Copy |
|--------|-------------|----------|
| Primitives | Independent (value copied) | Independent |
| Immutable references | Safe (cannot be modified) | Safe |
| Mutable references | Shared (dangerous) | Independent (safe) |
| Performance | Fast | Slower (creates new objects) |
| Use case | When all nested objects are immutable | When nested objects are mutable |

### What is the best approach in production?

Design objects to be immutable. Records, final fields, and unmodifiable collections eliminate the need for deep copying entirely.

```java
// Immutable design: no deep copy needed
public record AccountSummary(
    String accountId,
    BigDecimal balance,
    Address address  // Address should also be a record (immutable)
) {}
// Sharing references is safe because nothing can be modified.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Effective Java Item 11: override clone judiciously. Prefer copy constructors over Cloneable.
- In JPA, deep copy matters for detached entities: modifying a detached entity's nested collection can cause unexpected merge behavior.
- Apache Commons Lang provides SerializationUtils.clone() for serialization-based deep copy.
